                <UserObject label="" tooltip="Suppose you want the first $100 that flows into this &#39;FlowObject&#39; to go into a Container called Savings, and then after that, if anything remains, you want to put 40% of what remains into the Indulgences container and 60% of what remains into the Quality of Life Container.&#xa;&#xa;If $500 flows into this splitter, $100 would go into Savings leaving $400. Then, $160 would go to Indulgences and $240 would go to Quality of Life.&#xa;&#xa;Another example would be if $20 flowed in, all $20 would go to savings, and none would go to Indulgences or Quality of life since the first rule was that the first $100 goes to Savings.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;Translating these examples to what this looks like in the database, there would be two SplitterRule entries attached to the FlowObject. The first would be of type &#39;Static&#39;, and would have one RuleSet entry tied to it. The single RuleSet entry would have a value of $100, and a destination of Savings. The second SplitterRule would be of type percentage, and it would have two RuleSet entries.  One would have a value of40 with a destination of Indulgences, and the other would be a value of 60  with a destination of Quality of Life" id="98">
                    <mxCell style="shape=table;startSize=0;container=1;collapsible=0;childLayout=tableLayout;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=11;fontColor=default;labelBackgroundColor=default;" parent="1" vertex="1">
                        <mxGeometry x="640" y="550" width="210" height="120" as="geometry"/>
                    </mxCell>
                </UserObject>
